/**
This layout algorithm produces tree map layouts.
## Layout Style
Tree maps are suitable to visualize hierarchical data structures. They use nested rectangles to do so, where the size of
the rectangles encodes its weight. More generally, the area of a rectangle is proportional to a specific dimension of
data associated to it.
Tree maps can, for example, be used to show a file structure on a hard disk, where the dimension of data is the size of
the files/folders. There are many other use cases where the tree map can show the distribution of the dimension of data
among the entities in a hierarchy.
## Concept
{@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout} offers implementations of the well-known treemapping-algorithms {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SLICE_AND_DICE Slice-and-Dice} and {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SQUARIFIED Squarified}.
These _tiling_ policies divide a rectangle with a given size into multiple sub-rectangles. Speaking in terms of graphs this
means that nodes are placed in a nested fashion such that child nodes are placed inside the parent node.
Importantly, __node sizes are changed__ by this layout algorithm to show the proportional weight of a node in the map. The weight is specified by
registering a {@link yfiles.IDataProvider} with key {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.NODE_WEIGHT_DP_KEY} with the input
graph.
## Features
The input can be specified in two ways:
1. As a _hierarchic grouping structure_ that defines the nesting. Note that for group nodes, the specified insets are considered (see
{@link yfiles.GroupingKeys.GROUP_NODE_INSETS_DP_KEY}). If the graph is a tree graph, then the grouping structure is
ignored - see second input variant. To continue using the hierarchy information of the grouping structure on a tree
graph, one can temporarily hide the tree edges before running the layout.
1. As a _directed tree graph_. The parent-child relation is directly taken from the graph's tree structure. If the graph also contains group
nodes, the hierarchy relation induced by them is ignored. If necessary, an undirected tree can be (temporarily) directed
prior to the layout run by using
{@link yfiles.TreeAlgorithm.directTree}. The tree needs to be directed from the root (top-level hierarchy) to the children
(lower-level hierarchies). Note that a forest graph is no valid input.
The offered tiling policies produce drawings with a quite different style. {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SLICE_AND_DICE}
offers results that have a stable, clear ordering but the rectangles have a high aspect ratio. On the other hand, {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SQUARIFIED}
tries to create rectangles close to a given aspect ratio - in consequence, the ordering of neighbor nodes might not be
clearly predictable.
## Note
Due to insets specified at parent nodes of different hierarchies and the inter-node padding defined by the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.spacing spacing},
nodes that have the same actual weight can get slightly different sizes - especially if they are on different hierarchy
levels. This is necessary to satisfy the specified distances and insets. If exact same sizes throughout hierarchies is a
requirement, set the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.spacing spacing} to `0` and do not specify insets for the group nodes that have children (if input is
specified as grouping structure).
## Note
Edges are generally not routed by this layout algorithm. If there are any, their path will be reset such that they
become straight lines. If defining the input as a tree graph, edges can be removed after the layout run to get a clean
tree map drawing without any edges across the map. If defining the input via grouping, no edges are necessary
whatsoever.
__Warning:__ This layout algorithm does not consider {@link yfiles.NodeHalo}s assigned to nodes.
[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/TreeMapLayout).*/
export class TreeMapLayout {
/**
Initializes a new instance of the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout} class with default settings.
@param [options] The parameters to pass.*/
constructor(options?: {
/**
The tiling policy that defines how the input is divided into sub-rectangles. This option sets the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.tilingPolicy} property on the created object.*/
tilingPolicy?: yfiles.TilingPolicy | yfiles.TilingPolicyStringValues;
/**
The {@link yfiles.IComparer} that defines the order in which child nodes are placed inside their parent node. This option sets the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.nodeComparer} property on the created object.*/
nodeComparer?: yfiles.IComparer<any>;
/**
The minimum size (height and width) a node in the tree map must have. This option sets the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.minimumNodeSize} property on the created object.*/
minimumNodeSize?: yfiles.YDimension;
/**
The desired dimension of the root rectangle into which all nodes are placed. This option sets the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.preferredSize} property on the created object.*/
preferredSize?: yfiles.YDimension;
/**
The target aspect ratio of the {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SQUARIFIED squarified tiling policy}. This option sets the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.aspectRatio} property on the created object.*/
aspectRatio?: number;
/**
The spacing between nodes of the same hierarchy. This option sets the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.spacing} property on the created object.*/
spacing?: number;
});
/**
Arranges the given input graph as a tree map.
To create meaningful tree maps, the graph should either contain hierarchical grouping information or be a tree graph
that defines the hierarchy.
## Note
The given graph will not be copied during the layout process and the layout will be immediately applied to the given
graph. Node sizes will be changed to create the tree map layout.
@param graph the input graph*/
applyLayout(graph: yfiles.LayoutGraph): void;
/**
Data provider key for specifying the weight of the nodes
The weight of a node defines the area assigned to the node inside the available parent area with respect to the other
child nodes. Child nodes with the same weight get the same area, they do not necessarily get equal width and height
values.
All leaf nodes (nodes without further children, including empty group nodes) must have a weight greater than
`0`. Non-leaf nodes do not need to have a weight. They will basically get the sum of the weight of their children. If they
have a weight on their own, some free space is generated inside the representative rectangle (i.e. the parent node is
larger than the combined children).
## Note
Due to insets specified at parent nodes of different hierarchies and the inter-node padding defined by
{@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.spacing}, nodes that have the same actual weight can get slightly different sizes.
[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/TreeMapLayout%23NODE_WEIGHT_DP_KEY).
@const*/
static NODE_WEIGHT_DP_KEY: yfiles.NodeDpKey<number>;
/**
Gets or sets the tiling policy that defines how the input is divided into sub-rectangles.
@throws {@link yfiles.Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if an unknown policy is specified*/
get tilingPolicy(): yfiles.TilingPolicy;
set tilingPolicy(value: yfiles.TilingPolicy | yfiles.TilingPolicyStringValues);
/**
Gets or sets the {@link yfiles.IComparer} that defines the order in which child nodes are placed inside their parent node.
@throws {@link yfiles.Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if the given {@link yfiles.IComparer} is `null`*/
get nodeComparer(): yfiles.IComparer<any>;
set nodeComparer(value: yfiles.IComparer<any>);
/**
Gets or sets the minimum size (height and width) a node in the tree map must have.
If the {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.NODE_WEIGHT_DP_KEY weight data} induces that a node would get a width/height smaller than specified by the minimum size to fit into
the
{@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.preferredSize preferred size}, then the output size will be increased until all nodes fulfill the minimum size requirement. This
scenario will mostly occur if the weights of nodes have a large range or if the specified preferred output size is
small.
@throws {@link yfiles.Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if the width or height of the given size is smaller than `5` or if the given minimum size is `null`*/
get minimumNodeSize(): yfiles.YDimension;
set minimumNodeSize(value: yfiles.YDimension);
/**
Gets or sets the desired dimension of the root rectangle into which all nodes are placed.
If it is possible to arrange all nodes such that they fit into the preferred size, then the final result will exactly
have this size. However, satisfying the specified {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.spacing spacing} and {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.minimumNodeSize minimum node size} has higher priority. This means that the
final output might become larger if necessary. On the other hand, it will never be smaller, even if it would be possible
to generate a smaller result.
@throws {@link yfiles.Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if the given size is `null` or if its width/height value is less than or equal to `zero`*/
get preferredSize(): yfiles.YDimension;
set preferredSize(value: yfiles.YDimension);
/**
Gets or sets the target aspect ratio of the {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SQUARIFIED squarified tiling policy}.
The aspect ratio must be greater than or equal to `1`. A ratio of `1` means that the node rectangles should ideally be
squares. The orientation of the rectangles does not matter. For example, a ratio of `2` means that rectangles with an
aspect ratio of `1:2` or also `2:1` are desired.
This setting affects the ratio of a single node rectangle. To create a tree map with a certain overall aspect ratio, the
{@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.preferredSize preferred size} can be specified accordingly.
__Warning:__ This setting only has an effect if {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SQUARIFIED} is specified as {@link yfiles.TreeMapLayout.tilingPolicy tiling policy}.
@throws {@link yfiles.Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if the given aspect ratio is smaller than `1`
@see {@link yfiles.TilingPolicy.SQUARIFIED}*/
get aspectRatio(): number;
set aspectRatio(value: number);
/**
Gets or sets the spacing between nodes of the same hierarchy.
This spacing enables to insert a padding between all nodes/rectangles on the same hierarchy. It is not inserted between
a group node (border) and its children - use group node insets for that use case.
@throws {@link yfiles.Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if the given spacing is negative*/
get spacing(): number;
set spacing(value: number);
static $class: yfiles.Class<yfiles.TreeMapLayout>;
private $_TreeMapLayout: 0;
/**
Whether the given object is an instance of this type.
@param o The object to test.*/
static isInstance(o: any): o is yfiles.TreeMapLayout;
}
export interface TreeMapLayout extends yfiles.YObject, yfiles.ILayoutAlgorithm {
}
/**
The {@link yfiles.TreeReductionStage} temporarily reduces general graphs to trees.
## Layout Style
This stage prepares a non-tree graph such that it can be processed by a tree layout algorithm.
## Concept
This {@link yfiles.ILayoutStage} works in three steps:
1. Remove some edges to get a tree or forest from the graph
1. Invoke the {@link yfiles.LayoutStageBase.coreLayout core layout algorithm} which supports tree graph handling
1. Reinsert all previously removed edges
1. Optionally route these edges using the non-tree edge router and place their labels using the non-tree edge labeling
algorithm.
## Features
The routing of the temporarily hidden non-tree edges can be customized by specifying an {@link yfiles.TreeReductionStage.nonTreeEdgeRouter edge routing algorithm} for those
edges. Similarly, the placement of edge labels of non-tree edges can be delegated to a custom
{@link yfiles.TreeReductionStage.nonTreeEdgeLabelingAlgorithm edge labeling algorithm}. As default, both custom algorithms are not specified and, thus, non-tree edges are not routed
and edge labels not placed.
This stage is also able to handle multi-parent structures, i.e., structures of multiple nodes that share the same
predecessors as well as the same successors. More precisely, if the specified {@link yfiles.LayoutStageBase.coreLayout core layout algorithm} supports
multi-parent structures (see {@link yfiles.TreeLayout.multiParentAllowed}) and option {@link yfiles.TreeReductionStage.multiParentAllowed} is enabled, this stage does not hide such structures, i.e., the multi-parent
structures are passed to the {@link yfiles.LayoutStageBase.coreLayout core layout algorithm}.
Non-tree edges can also be bundled together such that their common parts are to some degree merged into a bundled part.
The edge bundling can be specified by means of method {@link yfiles.TreeReductionStage.edgeBundling}.
## Note
If there are edges between group nodes, the {@link yfiles.ClassicTreeLayout} may throw a
{@link yfiles.Exception InvalidGraphStructureError}-Exception. Such exceptions can be prevented by temporarily removing those edges.
[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/TreeReductionStage).*/
